# Tested theorems: custom generators

A theorem proved `by test N` is checked on N cases drawn for its binders by type. Random values
rarely hit the cases that matter: random `Text` almost never collides, so laws about names wrapped
their binder in a hand-built input (`Codebase.Spec.dotted`, `Bindings.Spec.crowded`, task 1cbfdd30).
Now `by test N using g` draws each case as `g` applied to a random `Int`, one generator per binder
in order ([D135](../../docs/DECISIONS.md)). Written in the order of the process (D92); the laws ran red
before `using` existed.

## Extensions

| Term | URI |
| --- | --- |
| `req` | `org.dxos.deus.req@1.0` |

## Requirements

```mdl
req R-1: by test N using g draws every case from g applied to a random Int
  by: Testing.Spec.usingDrawsFromGenerator | Testing.Spec.withoutGeneratorDrawsByType
```

```mdl
req R-2: a counterexample prints as the generator applied to its seed, so it can be evaluated again
  by: Testing.Spec.counterexampleNamesTheSeed
```

```mdl
req R-3: a generator is a dependency of the theorem, and a name after using that is not a definition is an error
  by: Testing.Spec.generatorIsADependency | Testing.Spec.unknownGeneratorRejected
```

```mdl
req R-4: using names one generator per binder
  by: Testing.Spec.generatorCountChecked
```

A statement `P -> Q` reads `implies P Q`, which is strict, so a case that failed `P` still evaluated `Q`:
`n != 0 -> n / n == 1` divided by zero (task 7ef8bf45).

```mdl
req R-5: by test checks the conclusion only on cases that meet every hypothesis, and fails when no case does
  by: Testing.Spec.hypothesesFilterCases | Testing.Spec.vacuousTheoremFails
```

## Theorems

The laws are the definitions under `Testing.Spec`: `deus ls Testing.Spec.` lists them and `deus view` prints them.
